{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<table class="table">
    <thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Template use</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>@model Page pkg.Page</code></td><td>Named root value</td><td><code>{{`{{ Page.Title }}`}}</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@dot pkg.User</code></td><td>The current dot value</td><td><code>{{`{{ .Name }}`}}</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@func userByID pkg.UserByID</code></td><td>Declared template function</td><td><code>{{`{{ (userByID 2).Name }}`}}</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@component Button pkg.Button</code></td><td>Project vocabulary for a typed root</td><td><code>{{`{{ Button.Label }}`}}</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@gen time pkg/timefuncs</code></td><td><a href="{{ goCluePath "/generated-helpers" }}" hx-get="{{ goCluePath "/generated-helpers" }}" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">Generated helper namespace</a></td><td><code>{{`{{ time.Format Page.At "15:04" }}`}}</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Runtime side</h2>
<p><code>@model</code> is the recommended convention for page or fragment data. It is not a magic keyword: any non-special annotation with a name and type becomes a typed root. The editor learns the type from the annotation, but runtime rendering still needs a value registered under that name.</p>
<p><code>@dot</code> is the contract for normal dot data. Use it when your application executes a template with <code>tmpl.Execute(w, value)</code> or passes dot into a child template.</p>
<p>Custom annotation names such as <code>@interaction</code>, <code>@component</code>, or <code>@struct</code> let a project describe framework or application-provided names without go-clue depending on that framework.</p>

<h2>Functions can return complex values</h2>
<pre><code>{{`{{ with firstUser }}
    {{ .Name }} - {{ .Status }}
{{ end }}

{{ (userByID 2).Email }}`}}</code></pre>

<h2>Go-side annotations</h2>
<p>Three comments in Go code describe template functions, so templates do not have to repeat <code>@func</code>. go-clue reads them statically and never runs your code. The old <code>//go-doc:</code> prefix works too.</p>
<table class="table">
    <thead><tr><th>Comment</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>//go-clue:funcmap</code></td><td>Above a func or var that holds a static <code>template.FuncMap</code> literal. Its entries become functions of every template.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>//go-clue:sig func(file string) string</code></td><td>Above a function, a FuncMap entry, or a closure assignment such as <code>funcs["url"] = func() ...</code>. The signature templates call it with. Use one line per call form.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>//go-clue:provider "github.com/acme/ui"</code></td><td>In a Go file of the project. Also index the go-clue annotations of that dependency package; end the path with <code>/...</code> for a subtree.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<pre><code>//go-clue:funcmap
func TemplateFuncs() template.FuncMap {
    return template.FuncMap{
        //go-clue:sig func(file string) string
        "asset": asset,
    }
}</code></pre>
</article>

